607: ## 9. Inherited discipline, stated so no seat has to infer it
608: 
609: - **Harness, LIVE**: every seat executes and prints `/usr/bin/python3 --version`,
610:   `/usr/bin/python3 -c "import sympy; print(sympy.__version__)"`, and `shasum -a 256 /usr/bin/python3`, with the
611:   first command likewise `/usr/bin/python3 --version`. **All three name the interpreter absolutely — the same binary
612:   C3 invokes.** *(`$(command -v python3)` resolved through the seat's PATH and was nowhere constrained to be
613:   `/usr/bin/python3`, so C3's claim that C5 pins its interpreter was true only where the two happened to coincide.
614:   Both V26 seats found it.)*
615:   Transcribing expected values fails `C5_HARNESS_PINNED`. *(`sympy.__version__` was written as though it were a
616:   shell command; run literally it fails with "command not found", so C5 as frozen could not be executed as frozen
617:   and each seat had to either fail it or silently substitute something that worked.)*
618: - **No transcription, anywhere.** Transcribing expected values fails the control that required them. **This applies
619:   to C1 as well as C5**: §2a prints the expected digests in this same document, so **a digest copied from §2a rather
620:   than computed from the file's bytes in the seat's own run fails `C1_SOURCE_IDENTITY`.** *(The anti-transcription
621:   rule existed but was scoped to one control, while C1's artefact — "the printed digests" — sat next to the answers.)*
622: - **Access proof** (Duho's 2026-09-04 20:48 rule): any seat auditing a pinned source prints
623:   `ACCESS_SHA=<full 64 hex of that file>` as its first line. **No proof, no verdict** — the report is quarantined,
624:   exactly as `nm_referee_dispatch.sh` does.
625: - **Path list**: every seat prints every path it opened. **IN SCOPE:** this lane's own reading tree
626:   (`../bhu-reading-20260823/sources/`); **this preregistration**; **the committed script
627:   `r3d_c3_deletion_probe.py` and the JSON input the seat writes for it**; **the pinned interpreter `/usr/bin/python3`,
628:   executed and hashed under C5 and invoked by C3, together with every file that interpreter loads while executing
629:   the mandated commands — its standard library, the installed sympy and mpmath packages, and their dependent shared
630:   libraries**; **`/usr/bin/shasum`, executed under C1, C3 and C5, together with the perl interpreter and libraries it
631:   loads**; and **the seat's own working directory and artefacts**. **GENERAL RULE, which the list above instantiates
632:   and which governs any path the list omits: a path opened as a necessary consequence of executing a command this
633:   document mandates is `IN_SCOPE` by that fact, printed and marked under this clause.** *(kimi V29, confirmed here by
634:   execution: the mandated sympy import opens 62 directories, none named, so a conforming seat marked each
635:   `OUT_OF_SCOPE` and failed C5b on every run. This clause had been repaired by adding one path at a time — V20, V27,
636:   V28 — and failed each time at the next unnamed path. `shasum` and its perl were the next one, found by tracing
637:   rather than by a gate. The rule now follows the mandate, not the list.)* *(kimi
638:   V27: C5 commands the seat to hash the interpreter and C3 to run it, yet no scope clause named it and "any path not
639:   named above" is `OUT_OF_SCOPE` — so a conforming seat failed its own C5b on every run, deterministically. The V20
640:   C5b defect, one path over.)* **OUT OF SCOPE:** another lane's files, and any path not named above. *(The rule previously
641:   named only the reading tree as in-scope, while the design itself requires a seat to open this document, the probe
642:   and its input — so every mandated read would have been marked `OUT_OF_SCOPE` and failed C5b. A control that fails
643:   on the paths its own design requires is not a scope rule.)* That distinction is stated
644:   because getting it wrong once already produced a spurious `UNDETERMINED` (R3B §5).
645: - **Stall guard**: every symbolic operation under a 120-second cap. On timeout print `SYMBOLIC_TIMEOUT` and execute
646:   **the fallback row below** under a second 120-second cap. If the row is absent, times out, or does not decide its
647:   proposition, file `DYM_SOURCE_BLOCKED` and **do not pass the affected control**.
648: 
649:   | symbolic operation | fallback | domain and sample | precision | decision threshold | proposition decided |
650:   |---|---|---|---|---|---|
651:   | solve `g_tt(r)=0` for horizon radii | bracketed root-finding on detected sign changes — bisection on each bracketing interval, or `mpmath.findroot` seeded from the bracket | `r/r_g ∈ [1e-3, 1e3]`, 10⁴ log-spaced samples | `mp.dps=30` | root accepted if a sign change brackets it and `abs(g_tt) < 1e-20` there | "the metric has ≥1 horizon for given (M, r₀)" |
652:   | `r→0` limit of curvature invariants | direct evaluation at `r = 10^-k`, `k = 1…20` | as listed | `mp.dps=30` | finite if `abs(value) < 1e6` and monotone-convergent over the last five `k` | "the core is regular" |
653:   | **free-symbol survival** (condition 3) | expand the final expression into a frozen expression tree and traverse every node once, substituting only §2b constants | the whole expression; no sampling | exact, symbolic | **PASS iff the printed sorted set of free symbols contains no non-§2b symbol**; FAIL if it contains one | "no free normalisation survives" |
654:   | extremise the mass over the core scale | grid minimisation then local refinement | `r₀ ∈ [1e-3, 1e3] r_g`, 10⁴ log-spaced | `mp.dps=30` | positive floor accepted if the grid minimum is `> 0` and stable to `1e-6` relative under a 2× refinement | "a positive minimum mass exists" |
655: 
656:   *(The fallback was previously called "named" while naming nothing, so a timeout reached an instruction that could
657:   not be executed and the run could stall before its own second timeout.)*
658: - **Seat split**: if the two blind seats return **different terminal classes of any kind** — scientific,
659:   `DYM_SOURCE_BLOCKED` or `R3D_NO_CLASS` — **or the same scientific class with different `C6_BREAKER_TEST`
660:   outcomes, or with different reported floor formulas or values (classes 1 and 2), or different reported families
661:   (class 4)** *(reachable because conditions 2 and 4 are bounded by each seat's own C2 artefact and no rule forces
662:   the two ledgers to agree — so two control-clean seats could file one class while one certifies a counterexample
663:   and the other does not)* — **the third seat adjudicates exactly that split from the printed
664:   artefacts**, **re-executing any blocked read once before ruling**; its class is filed **only if it agrees with one
665:   of the two**. If all three differ, or the third seat cannot decide, file `DYM_SOURCE_BLOCKED`. **Every terminal
666:   path files exactly one declared class.** *(The rule previously engaged only on "different scientific classes", so
667:   one seat scientific and one blocked was a terminal disagreement that convened nobody and filed nothing.)*
668: - **Control status vocabulary — every control emits exactly one of its three exact tokens**, never a bare PASS:
669:   `C0_REACHABILITY=PASS|FAIL|NOT_RUN`, `C1_SOURCE_IDENTITY=PASS|FAIL|NOT_RUN`,
670:   `C2_COMPLETION_LEDGER=PASS|FAIL|NOT_RUN`, `C3_DELETION_PROBE=PASS|FAIL|NOT_RUN`,
671:   `C4_GR_BENCHMARK=PASS|FAIL|NOT_RUN`, `C5_HARNESS_PINNED=PASS|FAIL|NOT_RUN`,
672:   `C5B_PATH_LIST=PASS|FAIL|NOT_RUN`, `C6_BREAKER_TEST=PASS|FAIL|NOT_RUN`. **`NOT_RUN` is permitted only where this
673:   document explicitly makes that control unreached. A control IS explicitly unreached when an earlier control has
674:   already forced `DYM_SOURCE_BLOCKED` or `R3D_NO_CLASS` and the later control was never engaged: every such later
675:   control is recorded `NOT_RUN`, and every control that WAS engaged before the block carries its actual result.**
676:   *(An early terminal block — C1 finding a digest mismatch, C2 an `UNRESOLVED` row, a bounded procedure undecidable
677:   after fallback — previously left every later control's status unstated, while the global rule allowed `NOT_RUN`
678:   only where the document said so explicitly. It now says so explicitly.)* *(Most controls previously printed only a PASS token. A
679:   control that can only say PASS is not a control — this lane's own principle, applied to itself.)*
680: - **Unreached limbs**: controls recorded `NOT_RUN`, never as passes. **This exact underscore spelling applies to
681:   every unreached control throughout this document.**
