141: ## 4. Outcome classes — six declared: four scientific outcomes and two non-scientific terminal states
142: 
143: **Definition, so classes 1, 2 and 4 have a common discriminator:** a **positive floor** means a **strictly positive
144: greatest lower bound of the allowed mass set, whether or not that bound is attained**; a **unique floor** means the
145: unique infimum of that set. *(Both seats found this: "floor" alternated between a lower bound and an attained
146: minimum, so a solution set like `M ∈ (1,2) kg` sat in class 5 under one reading and class 1 or 2 under the other,
147: and two obedient seats could file differently on identical physics.)*
148: 
149: **How the four scientific classes partition the cases — a decision procedure, not four descriptions.**
150: An **admissible reading** is either the **completion-free derivation** (the printed relations with nothing added)
151: or **the printed relations plus exactly one admissible completion**. Each **consistent** admissible reading either **yields a
152: positive floor** — a strictly positive greatest lower bound of its **non-empty** allowed mass set, attained or not
153: — or **permits masses approaching zero**, meaning no positive lower bound follows from it. **An admissible reading
154: whose printed relations are mutually inconsistent has an EMPTY allowed mass set: it is neither of those, and it
155: goes in a third set `I`.** *(The dichotomy previously admitted no third case, so an inconsistent reading — a paper
156: whose printed relations contradict each other, which is not hypothetical in this corpus — belonged to neither `P`
157: nor `Z` and fell out of the partition entirely.)*
158: 
159: Let **`P`** be the admissible readings that yield a positive floor, **`Z`** those that permit zero, and **`I`**
160: those that are inconsistent — empty allowed mass set. **Every admissible reading lies in exactly one of `P`, `Z`,
161: `I`.** Then:
162: 
163: | condition | class |
164: |---|---|
165: | **LIMB A EXIT.** After the frozen census of §2, **a relation binding size to mass, or bounding the mass, was unreproduced from the stated inputs**, so limb B is never entered | **3** `DYM_NO_SIZE_MASS_RELATION` |
166: | **LIMB B ONLY — at least one relation IS printed.** `P` is empty and `Z` or `I` is non-empty: **a positive floor was unreproduced from the stated inputs** | **4** `DYM_NO_POSITIVE_FLOOR` |
167: | **`P` is non-empty** and the readings **disagree** — `P` holds two different floors, or **`Z` is non-empty**, or **`I` is non-empty** | **2** `DYM_FLOOR_UNDERDETERMINED` |
168: | **`P` non-empty, all of `P` agree on one floor, `Z` AND `I` both empty** — which requires the completion-free reading to be in `P` | **1** `DYM_FLOOR_DERIVED` |
169: 
170: **Note on the state that has no class of its own.** A floor that exists **only under completions, with every
171: completion agreeing**, was formerly class 5, `DYM_FLOOR_COMPLETION_DEPENDENT`. **That state still occurs and still
172: files — as `DYM_FLOOR_UNDERDETERMINED` (class 2) — and that is correct, not a place to hide it.** If the
173: completion-free reading yields no floor while completions yield 10 kg, the admissible readings **genuinely
174: disagree**: one says no positive bound follows, the others say 10 kg. Disagreement among admissible readings **is**
175: underdetermination, which is what class 2 records. **The seat must still report every reading and what it yields**,
176: so the completion-dependence is visible in the filing even though it no longer has its own label.
177: *(Retired by Duho's ruling — see §8e. The state is not homeless: it is named here and routed.)*
178: 
179: **Class 3 is reached ONLY from limb A; classes 1, 2 and 4 ONLY from limb B. They are therefore disjoint by the
180: limb that files them, not by any predicate — and classes 1, 2, 4 partition limb B exhaustively. NO precedence rule
181: is needed, and none is stated.**
182: 
183: > ### Why this boundary kept failing, stated as a finding rather than repaired again
184: >
185: > **`DYM_NO_SIZE_MASS_RELATION` and `DYM_NO_POSITIVE_FLOOR` are not two physical states.** If nothing is printed
186: > constraining the mass, then the completion-free reading imposes no lower bound, so it lies in `Z`, `P` is empty,
187: > and class 4's predicate is **satisfied**. **Class 3 entails class 4 — by meaning, not by wording.** "No relation
188: > printed" is a *reason* no floor follows; "permits zero" is the *consequence*. Cause and effect, not alternatives.
189: >
190: > **And they are different KINDS of thing.** Class 3 is **procedural** — limb A's exit, decided before any
191: > completion is considered. Class 4 is a **state predicate** over readings that include completions. **Mixing a
192: > procedural exit into a state partition is the category error**, and it is why three predicate-level repairs
193: > failed in succession: **you cannot separate by predicate two things that differ only in when you stopped
194: > looking.** The repair above therefore separates them by **limb**, which is the actual difference.
195: >
196: > **The structural alternative is to retire class 3** and fold it into class 4 with a required reason field
197: > (`NO_RELATION_PRINTED` / `RELATIONS_PERMIT_ZERO`). **That is not done here: retiring a class is Duho's ruling,
198: > not mine**, and it is flagged for him as the candidate structural fix if the limb separation does not hold.
199: >
200: > **This is attempt three on this specific defect.** If a gate finds this same overlap again, the third-failure
201: > rule fires: **stop, file a diagnosis, and wait** — no fourth repair. *(V11 carried one, and it was the defect: class 4's
202: "permit" was written as "no positive lower bound follows", which is **exactly** class 5's own precondition, so
203: every class-5 case also satisfied class 4 and class 4's precedence took it — **making class 5 unreachable**. The
204: repair that widened C6 to three positive-floor classes had left one of the three dead. Requiring **P to be empty**
205: for class 4 — no admissible reading rescues a positive floor — separates them, and the mixed case, where one
206: reading gives a floor and another permits zero, now lands in class 2 where the genuine freedom belongs.)*
207: 
208: 1. **DYM_FLOOR_DERIVED** — as partitioned above. Report the formula and value, **and test it against all five
209:    breaker conditions**; if it passes them, this is a counterexample to the pattern and the pattern record must be
210:    amended. **If any breaker condition FAILS under its stated decision rule, `DYM_FLOOR_DERIVED` is still filed**
211:    and the failed condition is reported with its artefact: **the floor stands, it is not a counterexample, and the
212:    pattern record is not amended.** *(A breaker condition failing on its own decision rule is a **substantive
213:    physics result, not a control failure**, and does not engage the control-clean rule of `R3D_NO_CLASS`.)*
214: 2. **DYM_FLOOR_UNDERDETERMINED** — as partitioned above. **This class also carries the completion-dependent state**
215:    formerly held by the retired class 5: a floor arising only under completions. **Report every admissible reading
216:    and what it yields**, including any that permits zero, and **name any completion a floor depends on**; report the freedom and **choose none**. **Where `P` holds more than one floor, C6 is evaluated SEPARATELY ON EACH
217:    floor in `P`, the per-floor artefacts are printed, and `C6_BREAKER_TEST=PASS` exactly when every per-floor
218:    evaluation satisfies all five decision rules.** *("The quantity the seat actually FILED" otherwise had no unique
219:    referent here, since the seat is told to choose none — two obedient seats could evaluate different floors, split
220:    C6 inside one class, and send a case with a scientific answer to `DYM_SOURCE_BLOCKED`.)* **C6 is RUN on this class** and its
221:    result reported: a surviving freedom is what condition 3 rejects, so it is expected to fail — but the failure
222:    must be **exhibited**, not assumed. **Record `C3_DELETION_PROBE=NOT_RUN` on this class WHEN THE RUN REACHES IT DIRECTLY: the probe's trigger is a
223:    unique floor surviving on an injected relation alone, and a run reaching this class directly files no unique
224:    floor — the seat chooses none — so the probe has no target. If this class was instead SELECTED BY THE RECOMPUTED
225:    PARTITION after a probe FAIL, C3 was engaged and the filing records `C3_DELETION_PROBE=FAIL` with the removed
226:    relation and the recomputation printed, as C3's own clause commands; a control that ran is never recorded
227:    `NOT_RUN`.** *(kimi V28: on the class 1 → probe FAIL → recompute → class 2 path, this clause commanded `NOT_RUN`
228:    while C3's commanded `FAIL` — two tokens for one control in one filing, and `NOT_RUN` false of a run in which
229:    the probe found circularity. The class-2 clause (V26) and the recomputation clause (V27) were each repaired
230:    pairwise and never traced against each other.)*
231:    **If `C6_BREAKER_TEST=PASS` results here instead — every per-floor
232:    evaluation satisfying all five decision rules while the readings still disagree — then EACH floor in `P`
233:    independently passes the breaker, and the study reports that outcome with all floors named. It is NOT a single
234:    counterexample and the pattern record is NOT amended on it, because no unique magnitude was fixed: what passed
235:    is a family, and the pattern concerns a construction fixing A magnitude. The result is reported as
236:    `C6 PASS under an underdetermined floor` and referred to Duho.** *(The class previously stated only what a C6
237:    FAIL meant here and left a reachable `PASS` with no stated consequence.)*
238: 3. **DYM_NO_SIZE_MASS_RELATION** — limb A's exit: **a relation binding size to mass, or bounding the mass, was
239:    unreproduced from the stated inputs** after the frozen census of §2 was completed.
240: 4. **DYM_NO_POSITIVE_FLOOR** — as partitioned above. Report the family. **Record `C3_DELETION_PROBE=NOT_RUN` on
241:    this class WHEN THE RUN REACHES IT DIRECTLY: no positive floor survives, so the deletion probe has no candidate
242:    to test; C0, C1, C2, C4, C5 and C5b remain reached and carry their actual results, and C6 is `NOT_RUN` because
243:    no positive floor was produced. If this class was instead SELECTED BY THE RECOMPUTED PARTITION after a probe
244:    FAIL — the stripped reading set leaving `P` empty — C3 was engaged and the filing records
245:    `C3_DELETION_PROBE=FAIL` with the removed relation and the recomputation printed; a control that ran is never
246:    recorded `NOT_RUN`.** *(The sibling of kimi's V28 finding, found by tracing its replacement against every clause
247:    that records C3's disposition rather than applying it to the quoted class alone: the recomputed partition can
248:    select class 4 as readily as class 2, and this clause carried the identical collision.)*
249:    *(The class named no control dispositions while §9 permits `NOT_RUN` only where the document says so explicitly
250:    — the sibling, one class over, of the limb-A gap codex found at V15.)* If the printed relations are mutually
251:    inconsistent, **report that a consistent solution, and hence a positive lower bound, were unreproduced from the
252:    stated inputs**, file here, and **reproduce the contradiction.**
253: 5. **DYM_SOURCE_BLOCKED** — a required pinned source **cannot be read, or its computed identity — **or the pinned C3 probe's** — does not match the
254:    frozen digest, or a required source-dependent proposition is `UNRESOLVED`, or a required bounded
255:    procedure remains undecidable after its specified fallback, **or a terminal seat split under §9 that no
256:    adjudication resolves to one declared class**.** *(§9 already commands class 5 on a three-way or undecidable
257:    split among control-clean reports; the enumeration did not describe that state, so the filed class's own
258:    definition was false of the run — the study would have reported "source blocked" when no source was blocked.
259:    This appends the state the routing already sends here; it does not change where anything routes.)* The study **waits**; this is not a
260:    scientific verdict and must never be reported as one. **This class exists because the gate found §7's
261:    "reports BLOCKED if not" had no class behind it, so the run would have stalled.**
262: 6. **R3D_NO_CLASS** — **only after ruling out `DYM_SOURCE_BLOCKED`:** if **no** evidence is unread or unresolved
263:    and, after applying the seat-split rule of §9, a required control still fails after two attempts **in any seat**,
264:    file `R3D_NO_CLASS`; **otherwise file `DYM_SOURCE_BLOCKED`.** *(Class 6 said only that a source "cannot be read",
265:    which is narrower than the failure it causes, so an unread source satisfied both classes.)* **A scientific class may be filed only from a seat report in which every reached
266:    control passed**; if the two seats return the same scientific class but **at least one** report is not control-clean —
267:    **or if, after a different-class split, the third seat's adjudication agrees with a seat whose report is not
268:    control-clean** — **the third seat re-runs each failed control of EACH report that is not control-clean, once per failed control. If any re-run control fails again, that is the persistent
269:    failure named above and `R3D_NO_CLASS` is filed. If every re-run control passes, that class is filed.**
270:    *(The rule previously covered "exactly one" clean report, so the zero-clean case — both seats agreeing on class,
271:    floor and C6 outcome while each fails a DIFFERENT control — convened no split, exhibited no persistently failing
272:    control, and fell through to `DYM_SOURCE_BLOCKED`, whose definition would have been false of it.)* *(The old wording said "in both seats", so a control failing persistently in one seat,
273:    with the seats otherwise agreeing, had no rule and two readings of the document diverged at the terminal step.)*
