779:D00019	L00019	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM= We give a comparative description of different types of regular static, spherically symmetric black holes (BHs) and discuss in more detail their particular type, which we
780:D00020	L00020	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM=suggest to call black universes. The latter have a Schwarzschild-like
781:D00021	L00021	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM=causal structure, but inside the horizon there is an expanding Kantowski-Sachs universe and a de Sitter infinity instead of a singularity. Thus a
782:D00022	L00022	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM=hypothetic BH explorer gets a chance to survive. Solutions of this kind
783:D00023	L00023	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM=are naturally obtained if one considers static, spherically symmetric distributions of various
784:D00024	L00024	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM=(but not all) kinds of phantom matter whose existence is favoured by
785:D00025	L00025	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM=cosmological observations. It also looks possible that our Universe has
786:D00026	L00026	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM=originated from phantom-dominated collapse in another universe and
787:D00027	L00027	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM=underwent isotropization after crossing the horizon. An explicit example
788:D00028	L00028	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM=of a black-universe solution with positive Schwarzschild mass is
789:D00029	L00029	EXCLUDED:NO_MASS_OR_SIZE_CONTENT	line contains no mass/size/core/metric relation or defining context	VERBATIM=discussed. 
790:D00030	L00030	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM= 1. Introduction 
791:D00031	L00031	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM= One of the long-standing problems in black hole (BH) physics is the existence of
792:D00032	L00032	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM=curvature singularities beyond the event horizon in the BH solutions
793:D00033	L00033	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM=obtained under the simplest and the most natural physical conditions
794:D00034	L00034	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM=(the Schwarzschild, Reissner-Nordström, Kerr and Kerr-Newman solutions of general relativity and their
795:D00035	L00035	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM=counterparts in many alternative theories of gravity). Singularities are
796:D00036	L00036	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM=places where general relativity (or another classical theory of gravity)
797:D00037	L00037	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM=does not work. Therefore, a full understanding of BH physics requires
798:D00038	L00038	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM=avoidance of singularities or/and modification of the corresponding
799:D00039	L00039	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM=classical theory and addressing quantum effects. There have been numerous
800:D00040	L00040	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM=attempts on this trend, many of them suggesting that a singularity
801:D00041	L00041	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM=inside the event horizon should be replaced with a kind of regular core. 
802:D00042	L00042	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM= In this paper, we discuss the possible geometry of classical nonsingular
803:D00043	L00043	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM=BHs, restricting ourselves to asymptotically flat static, spherically symmetric configurations. In Sec.  2. we enumerate and briefly describe different known types of
804:D00044	L00044	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM=regular BHs. The rest of the paper is devoted to their particular type
805:D00045	L00045	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM=which we suggest to call black universes, namely, those in which a
806:D00046	L00046	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM=possible BH explorer, after crossing the event horizon, gets into an
807:D00047	L00047	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM=expanding universe [ 1 ] . Such objects are naturally obtained if one
808:D00048	L00048	INCLUDED	candidate relation / defining or physical context	VERBATIM=considers local concentrations of dark energy in the form of phantom
